ice. I'd say common mundane boring => 1.\n\nImplementability: degree easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost or non-radical changes =4. Some changes reasonably feasible =3. Significant change expensive/difficult but not totally impossible =2. Totally infeasible =1.\n\nCreating standardized internal software library, integrate into all high-risk pipelines, host central dependency. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Maybe significant change? For R&D lab with multiple projects, integrating API across all pipelines is non-trivial but doable. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nAcceptability: common strategies that violate no norms =4. Somewhat uncommon unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities =3. Offends somewhat =2. Radically violates =1. This is standard compliance tooling, socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies. So 4.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal does unified logging framework to ensure technical compliance with Article 12. That's direct. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = contributes partial solution. Will unified logging solve logging mandate? It automates capture of event timestamps, input data, system state changes. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons... The proposal mentions timestamps, input data, system state changes. Might not cover all specific items like reference database, identification of natural persons. But generally captures logging. Probably will solve main problem? It ensures technical com