        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P2004.\n\nProposal: Unified Compliance Logging Framework. Standardized software library integrated into every AI pipeline to automatically capture and store required event logs according to Article 12. Engineering team develop mandatory internal API that records precise timestamps, system states, trigger events in tamper-evident format. Ensures uniform regulatory compliance across diverse R&D projects while significantly reducing manual burden.\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\nacceptability evidence sentence then score.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable\n1 = radically violates laws or sensibilities, or totally unacceptable business practice\n\nProposal is standard compliance logging framework. Acceptable. Common strategy. Likely 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDevelop mandatory internal API integrated into every AI pipeline. That's significant engineering effort across diverse R&D projects. Not trivial. Probably some changes, reasonably feasible? Might be significant change. Unified library for all pipelines is major. Could be 2? Or 3? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" vs \"significant change, or expensive or difficult\". Developing standardized library integrated into every pipeline is significant change, expensive. But not totally impossible. Likely 2.\n\nLet's pick 2? Could argue 3. Engineering team develop mandatory internal API... significantly reducing manual burden. Might be some changes. Hmm. I'd say implementability = 3? Let's think: It requires engineering team to develop library and enforce integration across all projects. That's non-radical? Maybe moderately difficult. The anchor: 4 easy low cost. 3 some changes reasonably feasible. 2 significant change expensive or difficult but not totally impossible. I think unified framework across diverse R&D is significant change. So 2.\n\nRelevance applicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related...\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources. Proposal directly addresses automatic recording of events logs per Article 12. So applies directly. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nIf you could do it, it would solve main problem. Capturing timestamps system states trigger events in tamper-evident format ensures uniform regulatory compliance. It addresses logging mandate. Probably solves main problem. Score 4? Might be partial? Article 12 also requires specific logging for point 1(a) Annex III etc. Framework aims to capture required event logs according to Article 12. Seems will solve. So 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nUnified framework is a tool/format/process. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements but not change relationship? Might be slightly paradigm stretching. Score 2.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but also ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nUnified compliance logging framework is common, mundane. Standard practice. Score 1.\n\nNow produce output echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nNeed order: acceptability, implementability, applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nProvide evidence sentence first then score.\n\nProbably format JSON? Instruction says return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could output lines.\n\nI'll output as key-value pairs.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2004\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nMake sure evidence is one sentence.\n\nEnsure one sentence each.\n\nProceed.",
