king introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged.\n\nThis proposal introduces shared library tool, but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? Might be just standard compliance. It minimizes engineering burden but still logging practice. I think 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools... but still treats compliance as logging practice. Score 2.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related. Proposal provides automatic standardized event logging for high-risk AI deployments, captures timestamps etc. Directly addresses logging mandate. So 4 = solves identified problem directly related.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? Logging library would meet Article 12 requirement for automatic recording. It ensures consistent compliance. Probably will solve main problem. Score 4? Could be partial because need specific logging of events relevant to risk etc. But captures timestamps and system events by default. Might be sufficient. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Hmm.\n\nThe mandate requires logs over lifetime, recording events relevant for identifying risk situations etc., and for point 1(a) Annex III minimum data. Unified framework captures timestamps and system events by default, but may not guarantee specific data points like reference database, input data match, identification of natural persons. So partial. So effectiveness