d. Reallocate small percentage of institutional overhead funding to support permanent technical team. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. That's level 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Need budget approval maybe not easy. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability: common strategies that violate no norms... Budget ringfence is common, doesn't offend. Level 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Evidence: Ring-fencing compliance costs is standard institutional practice and does not contravene laws.\n\nNovelty originality: degree rare but ingenious. This is common mundane boring. Originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. It treats compliance as logging practice with dedicated team, no change relationship. So 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance... Could be slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (tools, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice -> level 2. Hmm. Budget ringfence introduces new element but still treats compliance as logging practice. The definition: 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged.\n\nDoes ringfence introduce new element? It's a budgeting process. Might be standard. I'd say paradigm preserving: lab's way unchanged, just funding. Probably 1.\n\nCheck anchor: paradigm breaking 4 introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Not.\n\nParadigm stretching 3 changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...). No.\n\nSlightly stretching 2 introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Budget ringfence is a new element? Might be considered 