able legally. No violation. So 4? Common strategies that violate no norms. Could be 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... \n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nCentralized data provenance registry with MLflow+DVC is common practice. Not rare. Might be 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting? I'd say common. So originality =1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nThis proposal introduces tools MLflow/DVC for provenance but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements but still compliance as logging. Might be 2.\n\nAlternatively could be 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance. Hmm.\n\nThe description: versioned registry linking model iteration to reference databases... provides verifiable audit trail. This is standard practice for reproducibility. It's standard compliance/logging practice. Might be paradigm preserving =1.\n\nGiven \"slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\". That fits exactly: introduces MLflow/DVC tools. So 2.\n\nLet's pick:\n\napplicability evidence: The proposal directly targets traceability of reference databases and input data for Annex III high-risk systems, which is a core logging requirement. => score 4? Wait earlier I said 3. Let's reconsider anchored: 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Stated problem is meet logging mandate. Y = traceability for Annex III requirements without manual record-keeping. That is part of stated problem. So maybe 4.\n\nEffectiveness: It will retrieve exact database state associated with historical system match, contributing to but not fully covering automatic event logging over lifetime or period of use. Could be 3.\n\nHmm.\n\nLet's decide final:\n\napplicability evidence: The registry links model iterations to reference databases and validation input data, directly addressing Annex III traceability requirements under Article 12. => applicability 4\n\neffectiveness evidence: It provides verifiable lineage for validation data but does not automatically record runtime events such as use periods or verifier identities required by Article 12(1) and (3). => effectiveness 3\n\nimplementability evidence: Deploying MLflow with DVC is a reasonably feasible engineering effort that many labs already use for experiment tracking. => implementability 3\n\nacceptability evidence: Using standard open-source ML tooling for audit trails violates no legal norms or business sensibilities and is widely accepted in R&D. => acceptability 4\n\noriginality evidence: Centralized provenance with MLflow and DVC is a common, mundane reproducibility practice in AI labs. => originality 1\n\nparadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal adds versioning tools to compliance but keeps logging as a separate record-keeping practice rather than redefining R&D. => paradigm_relatedness 2\n\nReturn JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing id. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably output structured.\n\nLet's produce clear list.\n\n",
