ocusing resources. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 4? It would solve stated problem without regard for workability? The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Audit doesn't meet logging mandate itself. So partial. Score 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 solves identified problem directly related... 3 solves implied problem...\nAudit directly relates to deciding where to apply logging. Seems directly related. Score 4? It solves identified problem that is directly related: avoid wasteful application. That is part of problem of how to meet mandate with scarce resources. I'd say 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): easy to implement at low cost...\nForm committee, systematic review. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies violate no norms. Score 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably list:\n\nP1513\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality:1\n\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nMake sure order listed: originality, paradigm_relatedness, effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide JSON? Probably plain text. Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\"...\n\nI'll output structured.\n\nLet's craft evidence sentences:\n\noriginality_evidence: Systematic risk classification audits are a standard compliance response routinely expected from labs facing AI regulation.\noriginality:1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal treats compliance as a filtering exercise and leaves the lab’s R&D workflow and its relationship to logging unchanged.\nparadigm_relatedness:1\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Correctly identifying high-risk