y: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 solves identified problem directly related. 3 solves implied problem related. The proposal applies to monitoring requirements. It directly addresses monitoring aspect of Article 12. Maybe 4? Solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Stated problem: meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This adapts R&D to use logs. So applicable. I'd say 4? Might be 3 because it solves implied problem of using logs for safety rather than just compliance. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal says directly satisfying monitoring requirements of Act. Monitoring is part of logging mandate. So applicability 4.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nNeed format: Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" then \"<subscale>\". Could output lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2016\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 2\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nImplementability evidence: Developing novel AI-driven anomaly detection models requires dedicated research staff, data infrastructure and validation time beyond routine logging. Score 2.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Using logs for safety research aligns with EU AI Act goals and raises no legal or ethical concerns. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The project weaves mandatory Article 12 logs into active R&D, turning compliance data into scientific inquiry rather than treating logging as a separate administrative task. Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Applying advanced anomaly detection specifically to regulatory logs for early-warning triggers is unusual and shows imaginative reuse of compliance data. Score 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The tools would improve monitoring and risk detection but do not by themselves guarantee the automatic recording and traceability required by Articles 12 and Recital 71