his proposal addresses a specific subset. Might be reasonable and will contribute.\n\nI think effectiveness = 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nIt does X = build audit trail module to get Y = meet record-keeping mandates. That's directly related.\n\nSo applicability = 4.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Building secure encrypted database linking queries to natural persons who verified results. Privacy concerns? Logging identities of natural persons may be sensitive under GDPR. But regulation requires it. Secure encrypted database is common. Probably acceptable. Maybe 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. Could be 4 = common strategies.\n\nI think building audit trail is common compliance strategy, violates no norms. So 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nIt says designed as pluggable component for any high-risk system deployment, fulfills mandates without requiring full redesign. That's non-radical changes? Some changes reasonably feasible. Pluggable component still requires integration, secure encrypted DB. Might be some changes, reasonably feasible measures = 3. Easy to implement at low cost? Maybe not easy; building secure encrypted DB linking queries is non-trivial. Probably 3.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious...\nCommon, mundane? Audit trail module for compliance is common practice. Not expressed before and ingenious? No. Common, mundane, boring =1. Could be interesting =2. Might be common.\n\nI'd say originality =1? Or 2? The proposal is specialized interface specifically for Annex III systems... It's a standard logging solution. Likely common. So 1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree preserves or modifies paradigm.\n1 = paradigm pre