gree to which idea can be easily implemented. Some changes, or reasonably feasible measures? Developing modular API integrates with existing ML pipelines, task systems engineering team. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Might be some changes. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Developing library is maybe not trivial. I'd say 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is standardized library for logging. It's still treats compliance as logging practice, introduces new tools. So slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice => 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious... Reference class set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Unified logging library is common, mundane. Score 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nCheck anchors: originality 1 = common, mundane, boring. Yes.\n\nNow produce output with id and each subscale evidence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale (applicability, effectiveness, acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality), in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON?