utomates core logging elements but does not guarantee capture of Annex III-specific items like reference database or verifier identity.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Yes logging middleware directly addresses compliance logging mandate. So 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Building middleware for logging is common compliance strategy. No violation. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Probably 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree easily implemented. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Python wrapper intercepts model calls writes metadata to centralized immutable DB. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Might be significant change? Building universal API layer for all prototypes is non-trivial but feasible. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Or 4 easy? Probably 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree preserves/modifies paradigm. 1=paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Middleware is new tool/process but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious. Unified logging middleware is common, mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 interesting? Logging middleware is fairly standard. I'd say 1 or 2. Common strategies. Originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nLet's pick originality 1.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating... give ... Probably output lines.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P1594\neffectiveness_e