te no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nImmutable data lineage is acceptable. 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nIntegrating version-control layer for datasets real time hashing? That's significant change, expensive. Probably 2 or 3. Might be significant change, expensive/difficult but not impossible. So 2? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" could be 3. Hmm real-time lineage pipeline is nontrivial. I'd say 2 = significant change, expensive or difficult.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated\n\nIt satisfies specific record-keeping mandates for Annex III systems regarding database references and input data matches. That directly addresses logging mandate. So effectiveness 4? Will solve problem without regard workability. Yes.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = ...\n\nIt directly applies to logging mandate. So 4.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating... For each subscale give ... Probably key-value pairs.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2641\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nMake sure evidence one sentence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nParadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a version-control and hashing tool for logs but keeps compliance as separate logging practice rather than redefining R&D.\nScore 2\n\nOriginality_evidence: Data lineage with hashes for model inferences is an interesting MLOps approach but not surprising or ingenious in AI compliance contexts.\nScore 2\n\nAcceptability_evidence: Immutable lineage logging complies with EU law and raises no legal, social or political objections.\nScore 4\n\nImplementability_evidence: Building a real-time version-control layer that hashes every input match and links it to inferences requires significant engineering and cost.\nScore 2\n\nEffectiveness_evidence: By recording database versions and input matches with hashes, the system directly fulfills Article 12(3)(b)-(c) record-keeping requirements for Annex III systems.\nScore 4\n\nApplicability_evidence: The pipeline targets the stated mandate to meet automatic logging and traceability obligations for high-risk AI systems.\nScore 4\n\nReturn.\n\n",
