g. So does it solve problem? It helps with documentation part of Recital 71 but Article 12 is about record-keeping logs. The proposal is about technical documentation generator. Might partially contribute? Effectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Could be 3.\n\nBut the stated problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Automated Technical Documentation Generator does not generate logs for events relevant to risk, substantial modification etc. It generates documentation from config/training logs. Does it meet logging mandate? No. So effectiveness maybe low. Effectiveness definition: degree to which idea will solve problem. If you could do it, would it solve main problem? Main problem is logging mandate. Documentation generator does not provide automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime per Article 12(1). So it's unrelated? It solves documentation, not logging.\n\nMaybe effectiveness =2 unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem (it probably will not work). Or applicability issue.\n\nLet's parse dimensions separately: relevance effectiveness and applicability.\n\nEffectiveness rating independent of workability. Would it solve stated problem if implemented? Stated problem is meet logging mandate. Automated doc generator does not produce event logs for system use, risk situations etc. So it would not solve main problem. Could be 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. Or 1 = solves unrelated problem.\n\nThe proposal addresses documentation up-to-date, which Recital 71 mentions, but Article 12 is logging. The problem statement says must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Documentation is part of compliance but not logging mandate per article. Might be partial solution? Hmm.\n\nRecital 71 mentions technical documentation should be kept up to date... Furthermore high-risk AI systems should technically allow automatic recording of events by means of logs. Article 12 is about record-keeping logs. So proposal is about documentation generator, not logs. It may be mis-targeted.\n\nThus effectiveness likely 2 or 1. It solves unrelated problem? Documentation is related but not logging mandate. Maybe applicability lower.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. 3 = solves implied problem related. The proposal generates living compliance document from config files, which relates to technical documentation requirement in Recital 71, an implied component of record-keeping compliance. So maybe applicability 3? It does X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem? Stated problem is logging mandate. Documentation is separate but linked via Recital.\n\nCould be applicability 2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates.\n\nI think best fit: effectiveness 2 (unreasonable/unlikely to solve logging mandate). applicability 3 (solves implied problem related to compliance documentation).\n\nOriginality: degree rare ingenious. Automated doc generator is common, mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 interesting? I think common practice. Score 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 paradigm breaking... 3 paradigm stretching... 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged.\n\nAutomated documentation generator is standard compliance practice. That's paradigm preserving. So 1.\n\nLet's produce output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nNeed order: acceptability, implementability, effectiveness, applicability, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nProvide lines.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nacceptability_evidence: The proposal automates internal documentation without collecting personal data or altering model outputs, which aligns with legal expectations and does not offend professional norms. acceptability 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Developing parsers and CI hooks to transform existing configs and training logs into PDFs or wiki pages is a reasonably feasible engineering effort with standard tooling. implementability 3\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Generating living technical documentation does not create the automatic event logs for system use, risk situations, or post-market monitoring required by Article 12(1)-(3). effectiveness 2\n\napplicability_evidence: The tool addresses the Recital 71 requirement to keep technical documentation up to date, an implied compliance need related to record-keeping even though it does not provide the mandated logs. applicability 3\n\noriginality_evidence: Automated documentation generation from model artifacts is a common DevOps practice and offers no ingenious or surprising twist. originality 1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The lab continues to treat compliance as a documentation task and does not change the relationship between R&D work and its logging obligations. paradigm_relatedness 1\n\nReturn rating.\n\n",
