        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rate proposal P2437.\n\nProposal: Unified Compliance Logging Framework. Internal API and wrappers automatically capture timestamps, input hashes, system states. Standardized telemetry layer for all high-risk AI prototypes. Build middleware layer for common model frameworks pipes essential event data to secure immutable database. Ensures consistency across teams. Removes manual burden while guaranteeing Article 12 compliance.\n\nRate each subscale.\n\noriginality evidence: one sentence. Need pick level 1-4.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious, imaginative, or surprising.\nCommon? Unified logging middleware for compliance is common, mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 = interesting? Might be common practice. I'd say common. So originality =1.\n\nEvidence: The proposal describes standard middleware telemetry and immutable logging commonly used for regulatory compliance.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\nParadigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. This is standard logging practice, treats compliance as logging practice. Could be slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. It introduces middleware layer, standardized telemetry. Might be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nText says removes manual burden from researchers while guaranteeing Article 12 compliance. That's standard. I'd pick 2? Could be 1 paradigm preserving. Hmm \"standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\". The middleware does change workflow? Might be slight change. I think 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nEvidence: It adds a reusable logging middleware but leaves R&D objectives and compliance relationship unchanged.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\nProblem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nUnified framework captures timestamps etc. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons... The proposal mentions timestamps, input hashes, system states. Might capture essential event data. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? Probably will contribute to solution but partial? It guarantees Article 12 compliance. Might be 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability.\n\nBut does it guarantee? It captures timestamps, input hashes, system states. Article 12(3) for point 1(a) systems requires specific data like reference database, identification of natural persons. Proposal doesn't explicitly mention those. Maybe partial.\n\nEffectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps but only partial). Could be 4.\n\nI'll pick 3: It provides automatic logging infrastructure that contributes substantially to traceability but does not specify coverage of all Article 12(3) data items.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related. Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Yes. So applicability 4.\n\nEvidence: Building a middleware that auto-captures events directly addresses the mandate to meet Article 12 logging requirements.\n\nimplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nSome changes, or reasonably feasible measures -> 3? Or significant change