lem.\nParadigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged =1\nSlightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice =2\nParadigm stretching: changes relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...) =3\nParadigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches =4\n\nThis is internal vetting process. Standard compliance practice? Risk categorization is fairly standard. It introduces review board. Probably paradigm preserving? Might be slightly stretching introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. I'd say 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nCommon, mundane, boring =1\nInteresting=2\nUnusual, interesting; shows some imagination=3\nNot expressed before AND ingenious...=4\n\nRisk categorization framework for AI Act is common. So 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nWorkability implementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes =4\nSome changes, or reasonably feasible measures =3\nSignificant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible=2\nTotally infeasible=1\n\nQuarterly review board with legal counsel and PIs, standardized rubric. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms =4\nSomewhat uncommon but do not offend =3\nOffends sensibilities somewhat=2\nRadically violates laws...=1\n\nThis is standard compliance governance. Acceptable. Score 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sente